# About the project 

`rustc_codegen_gcc` is alternative Rust *compiler backend*(compiler plugin), allowing users to compile Rust code using GCC instead of LLVM. 

During my GSoC work, I greatly improved the ability of `rustc_codegen_gcc`(`cg_gcc` for short) to compile Rust code, fixing numerous bugs. 

This allowed me to achieve the end goal of my proposal: building("bootstrapping") the entire Rust compiler using this new backend.

My work also involved huge improvements to the `cg_gcc` testing infrastructure, allowing for automated fuzzing, and ABI compatibility tests.

# My work

## Compilation bugs

A substantial amount of my work involved finding & fixing compiler bugs.

I will briefly outline them here, but I have [written up a larger article](FractalFir.github.io/generated_html/cg_gcc_bootstrap.html), discussing them in detail.

Miscompilations(cases where the compiler emits incorrect machine code) are especially hard to find. They are usually triggered by very obscure edge cases.

One of the bugs I discovered was caused by using structs containing the `u128` & `i128` types together with the packed attribute.

This bug prevented us from bootstrapping the Rust compiler with optimizations enabled. 

```rust
#[repr(packed(1))]
struct Buggy{
	a:u128,
	b:u8,
}
```

Those types(and only those types!) would cause the optimized code to segfault. Other types with such aligement(eg. `f128`), and other primitives(`u64`), would work just fine. 

The underlying cause of this bug turned out to be... a broken workaround for a *different* bug. Finding this issue required days of work - but only a 1 line fix. 

I have found many bugs exactly like this - small, hard to trigger, but with earth shattering impact. Fixing them was a necessary precondition for bootstrapping `rustc` with `cg_gcc`.

However, that raised an important point: we need better testing tools.

## Better ways to find such bugs 

The Rust ecosystem contains a wide variety of tools for testing and fuzzing the compiler. On such tool is `rustlantis`. 
This tool generates random(but well-defined) Rust programs, allowing us to compare the result of compilation with LLVM and GCC. 

This is an excellent way of finding bugs: it gives us concreate, UB-free programs, which demonstrate the bug in question.

The generated programs, however, tend to be fairly large(10k+ LOC!), and require reduction. 

During GSoC, I integrated `rustlantis` into the `cg_gcc` test suite, allowing for automatic fuzzing - and reduction of test cases.

Thanks to my work, `cg_gcc` can now automatically fuzz itself, leaving a set of reduced(~1K Loc) samples for the contributors to look over. 

This allows us to leave the the fuzzer running overnight, and go back to a set of reduced error cases. 

We can then look over those, and figure out what compiler bugs they trigger. This approach allowed me to find a GCC bug - a case where the code gets incorrectly optimized. 

```c
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stdio.h>
bool womky(void) {
  float val = 3.40e+38;
  float a = val * -(val * val);
  float b = val * a;
  return b * b > 0.0;
}
int main(void) { printf("%d", womky()); }
```

This function will print `1` without optimization, and `0` with optimizations enabled. 

We can see that GCC get's confused here, and thinks that the value of `b` can only be NaN:
```
Global Exported: a_4 = [frange] float [+Inf, +Inf] +-NAN
Global Exported: b_5 = [frange] float [] +-NAN
Global Exported: _3 = [frange] float [] +-NAN
```
This shows the beneifts of testing the GCC Rust compiler backend: it allows us to uncover bugs *both* in the Rust compiler, and in tools like `GCC`.

## ABI tests

When trying to bootstrap `rustc` on ARM, I encountered a crash in the proc-macro system. This crash was caused by an ABI bug, that only occurred on ARM. 

This motivated me to integrate the `abi-cafe` ABI tester into our test suite, allowing us to find ABI bugs, and track ABI regressions.

## DEC Alpha & m68K support

After successfully building the Rust compiler on x86_64, I started working on building it on other platform. 

I improved DEC Alpha support in a few crates, and fixed some m68k specific bugs in the Rust compler. Sadly, Dec ALPHA support could not get merged due to rules in the Rust project about target support. 

In order for me to merge code supporting DEC Alpha, it needs to be a tier 3 target, which requires a dedicated maintainers. 

This is not a burden I am willing or able to partake: I do not posses any DEC Alpha machines. 

# Work left to do

## Bugs
During my work, I have discovered a significant amount of bugs, some of which I have not been able to fix. 

Here is a list of them:
```
github.com/rust-lang/rustc_codegen_gcc/issues/689
github.com/rust-lang/rustc_codegen_gcc/issues/690
github.com/rust-lang/rustc_codegen_gcc/issues/691
github.com/rust-lang/rustc_codegen_gcc/issues/701
github.com/rust-lang/rustc_codegen_gcc/issues/704
github.com/rust-lang/rustc_codegen_gcc/issues/711
github.com/rust-lang/rustc_codegen_gcc/issues/752
```
The *vast majority* of the bugs in questions were caused by incorrect optimizations to floating point numbers. 
Many of those issues may need to be fixed on the GCC side, or may require new GCC flags(to preserve the semantics of Rust).

Fundamentally, the issue is twofold:
1. Some optimizations are incorrectly applied - this is a GCC bug, and breaks `C` float semantics.
2. Some optimizations are *valid* in `C`, but are not correct with Rust's more strict semantics. 

This, however, means that I can't really fix those bugs myself, at least not within a reasonable time frame.
Contributing complex GCC fixes requires a deeper knoweladge of that project, which I frankly do not posses. 

## M68k support
While my patches improved m68k support, there are still some things left to do.
The primary issue tracking that work can be found here:
https://github.com/rust-lang/rustc_codegen_gcc/issues/744

It contains detailed descriptions of all the steps required to build the Rust compiler on m68k.

Surprisingly, most of the changes ought to be made in 3-rd party code(support crates). The compiler-side support is more or less done.

Besides a small, albeit important detail. 

M68K Linux has some odd ABI requirements: it aligns 32 bit intigers(and pointers) to 2 bytes. It remains unclear if this is something the Rust spec allows for. 

The maintainers of distros supporting M68K are working on a move to 4-byte aligned 32 bit numbers, but... that will take time. 

# What code did and did not get merged

The vast majority of my patches got merged upstream, however, some things either could not be merged(DEC Alpha support) or did not get merged in time(ABI refactor of `cg_ssa`). 

# Technical challenges

The single biggest challenge in my work was the very limited and hard to use `libgccjit` API(the API we use to talk to GCC). 

Due to some technical choices(so called "jit recording"), `libgccjit` is very memory hungry, and does not free memory until compilation ends. 

For example, creating an integer type with a specified aligement(eg. `4`) will always allocate a new type:
```
#[test]
fn usize_eq_usize(){
    let ctx = Context::default();
    // Assert fails, cause both calls to `get_aligned` 
    // return different instances of the gcc_jit_type class. 
    assert_eq!(
      ctx.new_type::<usize>().get_aligned(8),
      ctx.new_type::<usize>().get_aligned(8)
    );
}
```

We can't free this data - it is *required* for JIT recording. 

This is highly detrimental to good developer experiences. 

The poor documentation of the Rust compiler was also a huge roadblock. The APIs are often hard to comprehend, have poor / or no documentation, making work considerably more difficult than it ought to be. 

All the `BuilderMethods` are based on components of LLVM. However, [none of them are documented on the Rust side](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/nightly-rustc/rustc_codegen_ssa/traits/builder/trait.BuilderMethods.html#tymethod.catch_pad).

This means that the *assumptions `rustc` makes* about those APIs are undocumented.

